WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:18.060
STUDENT: My question is in regards to mainline
Protestantism here in the states, and the four

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.669
factors

00:00:19.669 --> 00:00:22.810
you have laid out.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.650
Arguably, you know, the Episcopal Church, for
instance, meets-- has met a number of

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.669
those factors, and increasingly so

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:31.289
as it's declined.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:35.770
So how do you account for that? 
GORSKI: Oh, I got a very simple answer for this.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.290
Demography. 

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.640
There's a

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.590
wide, wide--

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.350
widely shared misunderstanding which is systematically
propagated by--

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.760
by certain

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.530
religious

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.080
movements within the United States-- Evangelicals--
that

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.670
the growth of Evangelicalism has to do with
the-- relative to the mainline Protestantism-- has to

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.180
do with the success of their

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.590
outreach or the character of their theology.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.690
Basically, it's all about 

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.390
average levels of female fertility.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.619
Even--

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:12.060
have, you know, on average-- Evangelical women,
for example, on average have, you know, 3.4,

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.040
3.5

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.260
children during their lifetimes, whereas mainline
Protestants,

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.980
you know, are well below replacement levels--
1.4,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:26.910
in more liberal Protestant congregations. Obviously, you know, 
to the degree to which religious reproduction

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.479
is partly about biological reproduction is
about that, to a large degree.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:34.490
People switch, but not as much as you might
think,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.360
over their lifetimes. They typically switch within--

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.649
within denominational families. The decline
in mainline Protestantism

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:43.950
is almost all about declining levels

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.939
of female fertility. Now,

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:50.770
that rate-- that points you to what the real
theological question is, which is, you know,

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:51.600
why do--

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.650
why do Evangelicals have more kids? 
And I don't have a--

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.989
you know, I don't have a great answer to that question,
but if you--

00:01:57.989 --> 00:02:00.159
you know, if you come up with one,

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:01.960
I guarantee you, you'll get a lot of attention. 

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.960
VOLF: How about the Episcopalians-- how are the Episcopalians

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.260
having this gaping hole-- I'm one-- but we've got
gaping hole between 12 and

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.299
35

00:02:12.299 --> 00:02:17.319
in our congregations. So it's not just the
low rates of fertility-- it's-- it's

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:20.479
lower rates of attraction of

00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:22.519
those who you give birth to, right?

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:27.379
So-- which is-- which is a sense of
issue of

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:28.689
the issue of both

00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:29.900
internal transmission

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.919
and ability to internally transmit,

00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:35.719
also. Philip, do you think that way at all?

00:02:35.719 --> 00:02:36.469
GORSKI: You know, I--

00:02:36.469 --> 00:02:39.499
you know, I haven't really looked at the numbers
really closely. I mean, one thing

00:02:39.499 --> 00:02:40.870
I can say is that,

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.060
you know, what you're describing is a fairly
typical

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:44.450
phenomenon--

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.739
you know, that

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:50.389
in mid-to-late adolescence, people leave.

00:02:50.389 --> 00:02:53.519
When they get married and then when they have
kids,

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:55.229
they tend to return.

00:02:55.229 --> 00:02:58.739
So, I mean, that's a-- that's a very common-- that's a
very common thing.

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:00.999
What might be true is that,

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:03.530
you know, that--

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.849
the kind of--

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:12.019
stricter churches, you know, Evangelical or Pentecostal--
stricter churches, which really have much

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:16.609
stronger for, you know, for theological
reasons, have-- find much stronger forms

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:17.249
of

00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:20.579
collective pressure on individuals to remain within
the fold,

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:24.919
may actually do a better job. That's why I say
I don't know the numbers, whether they do

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:27.419
a better job of actually keeping people

00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:31.309
active, you know, between the ages of, you know,
whatever, 15 and 18 or

00:03:31.309 --> 00:03:35.239
35 or 40. It may may well be that
they do, but, you know, whether that matters

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:36.930
or not a lot in the long run,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.539
again, is a--

00:03:38.539 --> 00:03:42.779
that's another question. Maybe it does. Maybe
it does in the sense that-- it could be that's

00:03:42.779 --> 00:03:46.319
part of the answer to the fertility problem, that those are--

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:50.819
that that's precisely the period of late adolescence
and young adulthood when people develop certain

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:55.709
kinds of longstanding attitudes towards sexuality,
marriage,

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:57.249
reproduction, and so on.

00:03:57.249 --> 00:03:58.209
That's just a

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:00.529
thought, I don't know if it's true.

00:04:00.529 --> 00:04:02.739
STUDENT: Can I push back just a little bit on--

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:06.709
the idea of fertility answering it all-- because
it's my understanding that the kind of cradle

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:08.480
of Protestantism

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.969
is fading, also, that that's sort of a corresponding

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:12.300
trend that

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.599
or-- so,

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:18.040
it seems to me that there has to be something
else accounting for it

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.709
given that they are meeting a number of these
factors--

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:26.149
or is it anticipating something that might then,
now, affect some of these flourishing,

00:04:26.149 --> 00:04:29.650
growing religions that they haven't yet faced,
and what's-- what's the x factor?

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:33.470
GORSKI: You know, it's conceivable, but, you know,
again I think that,

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.479
you know, I think-- there have been several studies
of this-- one particular quite recently,

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:40.729
within the last three or four years-- that,

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:46.269
you know, really find that almost, an extraordinary
amount of this sort of variation is just

00:04:46.269 --> 00:04:49.479
explained by pure demography. I mean, I can give you the site
afterward.

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:51.350
You know, whether or not 

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.990
that-- that's changing--

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.229
I mean somebody, you know--

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:57.810
you'd have to really sort of go and look at
the data to figure out whether that's the case. I--

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.589
it's not implausible, what you're saying. It's just a question
of whether it's true. 

